<main class="guide legal wrap" id="main">
  <header class="docs-header">
    <h1>Support</h1>
    <p class="lead">How to get help with this site's tools.</p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="contact">
    <h2 id="contact">Ask us</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>By email:</strong> <a href="mailto:{{ site.contact }}">{{ site.contact }}</a>. We answer as soon as we can, with no set time.</li>
      <li><strong>A bug or an idea:</strong> <a href="{{ site.github }}/h5p-toolkit/issues">open an issue on GitHub</a>. Issues are public: leave out personal data and files you cannot share.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Say what you asked the chat for, which content type it made, and what you saw.</p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="problems">
    <h2 id="problems">When something goes wrong</h2>
    <dl class="legal-list">
      <dt>The chat shows no activity</dt>
      <dd>Ask the chat to show it again. A card that says “No activity arrived” got nothing from the chat to play.</dd>
      <dt>“Your video could not be fetched from the chat”</dt>
      <dd>Its address in the chat may have run out. Choose the file from your computer when the page asks: it stays in your browser.</dd>
      <dt>The activity is bigger than the chat can save</dt>
      <dd>The chat saves up to a size it sets, about 8 MB in ChatGPT in November 2026. Above that, when your file plays from the chat, Download opens a tab of this site that fetches it, builds the <code>.h5p</code>, saves it and closes. When the page says to, right-click Download, or press and hold it, and open the link in a new tab: that tab saves it.</dd>
      <dt>Using the activity elsewhere</dt>
      <dd>The <code>.h5p</code> file plays in Moodle or any other H5P site. The <a href="/h5p/build/">build page</a> builds and plays a chat's source in your browser too.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

